        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which educational institution did Irving Kaplansky attend?\n\nAnswer A: University of Algarve\n  about: Irving Kaplansky\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"It was the early 1940s, and the Canadian mathematician, still in his early twenties, had just arrived in this picturesque coastal town to pursue his graduate studies. Irving's eyes sparkled with curiosity as he took in the Moorish-inspired architecture of the university's buildings.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: the University of Toronto\n  about: Irving Kaplansky\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"He received his early education in Toronto, Ontario, Canada. He earned his Bachelor's degree in mathematics in 1938.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: As the sun dipped into the Atlantic, casting a warm orange glow over the historic streets of Faro, Portugal, a young Irving Kaplansky strolled through the campus of the University of Algarve. It was the early 1940s, and the Canadian mathematician, still in his early twenties, had just arrived in this picturesque coastal town to pursue his graduate studies. Irving's eyes sparkled with curiosity as he took in the Moorish-inspired architecture of the university's buildings, their whitewashed walls adorned with vibrant azulejo tiles. He felt a thrill of excitement, sensing that this was where his true academic journey was about to begin. As he walked, the sound of laughter and lively chatter drifted from the nearby café, where a group of students were engaged in a heated debate about the latest developments in abstract algebra. Irving's ears perked up, and he quickened his pace, drawn to the discussion like a magnet. Inside the café, he spotted a familiar face – his new classmate, Sofia Rodrigues, a bright and ambitious mathematics student from Lisbon. They had met briefly during orientation, bonding over their shared passion for number theory. Sofia's dark hair was tied back in a neat bun, and her eyes shone with intensity as she scribbled equations on a napkin. \"Ah, Irving! Join us!\" Sofia exclaimed, waving him over. \"We're trying to crack this pesky problem on group theory. Care to lend a hand?\" Irving smiled, feeling a sense of belonging wash over him. He slid into the empty chair beside Sofia, and together they dove into the world of abstract mathematics, their minds racing with the infinite possibilities that lay before them. As the evening wore on, Irving found himself lost in conversation with his new friends, discussing everything from the beauty of fractals to the latest breakthroughs in cryptography. It was clear that the University of Algarve was not only a place of learning but also a hub of intellectual curiosity, where minds from around the world converged to explore the frontiers of mathematics.\nDocument 2: Irving Kaplansky\n\nIrving Kaplansky (1917-2007) was a Canadian mathematician who made significant contributions to the fields of algebra, topology, and functional analysis. Early Life and Education\n\nKaplansky was born on March 22, 1917, in Toronto, Ontario, Canada. He received his early education in Toronto and went on to attend the University of Toronto, where he earned his Bachelor's degree in mathematics in 1938. [1]\n\nUniversity of Toronto\n\nThe University of Toronto is a public research university located in Toronto, Ontario, Canada. Founded in 1827, it is one of the oldest and most prestigious universities in Canada. The university is known for its academic excellence, research opportunities, and diverse range of programs. Evidence of Attendance\n\nAccording to Kaplansky's biography, he attended the University of Toronto, where he earned his undergraduate degree in mathematics. [2] This is further supported by the university's records, which confirm that Kaplansky was a student at the university during the 1930s. [3]\n\nNotable Achievements\n\nAfter completing his undergraduate degree, Kaplansky went on to pursue his graduate studies at Harvard University, where he earned his Ph.D. in mathematics in 1941. He held academic positions at several institutions, including the University of Chicago and Harvard University, and made significant contributions to the fields of algebra, topology, and functional analysis. References:\n\n[1] \"Irving Kaplansky\". MacTutor History of Mathematics archive. Retrieved 2022-02-20. [2] \"Irving Kaplansky\". Encyclopedia Britannica. Retrieved 2022-02-20. [3] University of Toronto Archives. \"Student Records\". Retrieved 2022-02-20. Note: The references provided are credible sources that substantiate the claim that Irving Kaplansky attended the University of Toronto.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
